From: Justin Walsh <contact.me@thejustinwalsh.com>
Date: Wed, 07 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] server: POST /decide-batch reads many blocks over one shared prefix in one pass

jjava (mcp/decider_bonsai.py) reads each question in two option orders, each
read one HTTP request on the cached state: ~0.26 s of fixed cost per read
beside ~2.3 ms per processed token (Decision Index run, 2026-10-06, 86-row
fit). This reads ALL of a call's blocks in one request.

common/decide-batch.{h,cpp}: the prefix (the shared state) is decoded once on
a sequence of its own and kept between calls on request; every block runs on
a scratch sequence forked from it with llama_memory_seq_cp (attention KV:
shared cells, nothing copied; recurrent state: copy-on-write at the fork's
first token), so the blocks never see each other and each logits row is what
a lone request for prefix + block reads. The blocks of a wave go through
llama_decode together, in lock step slices, longest first. A group's blocks
(the two orders of one question) are decoded from their longest common
TOKEN prefix once and forked from there: sharing skips only identical
tokens, so it cannot change a row. A group larger than the forks is cut into
chunks. Scratch sequences are freed after every wave and again at the start of
every call; a failure (or a throwing row reader) leaves nothing behind (forks
freed, resident prefix dropped).

server: --decide-seqs N reserves N fork sequences and the prefix sequence
beside the slots (n_seq_max = n_parallel + 1 + N; one recurrent cell each,
~150 MiB for the 27B; needs --kv-unified, else the endpoint stays off), and
raises n_outputs_max to hold their rows. POST /decide-batch {prefix, groups,
token_ids, top_logprobs, keep_prefix, verify_tokenization, release} runs as a
task on the loop thread between decodes (declined while a decode yields, so
nothing else touches the context), tokenizes (strings, or token arrays as
/completion takes them), and answers each block's natural-log softmax over
the whole vocabulary: the top N and the exact value of every requested id.
Errors: 501 without --decide-seqs, 400 for a malformed body / a token outside
the vocabulary, 503 when the unified
pool has no free cells. /props reports decide_batch.{seqs,prefix_seq}.

tests/test-decide-batch.cpp (static CPU tree, a tiny random hybrid qwen35:
linear-attention layers carrying recurrent state beside full attention):
rows equal a cold single-sequence decode of prefix + block (largest
difference 2.4e-4 in log-probability, float32 order-of-sum noise; a wrong
fork moves a row by 0.76 or more, and two deliberate mutations of the fork
logic fail 18 and 6 of 140 checks), group order and duplicates change nothing,
shared heads are detected and decoded once, the prefix is reused when
resident or extended and reset otherwise, scratch sequences and the slots'
own sequences are left exactly as found, a failure leaves nothing behind.

Origin: ours. The mechanism (slots continuing one shared prompt through
llama_memory_seq_cp) is the one ggml-org/llama.cpp's server uses for its
decision models (server_decision_group_tasks / copy_prompt_to, PR #29818 and
#29831, MIT, as this tree). docs/DECIDE-BATCH.md.
